// All user-facing strings. This file deliberately has no include guard: it is an
// X-macro list, expanded into the Msg enum (by tethernet/common/i18n.h) and the
// message table (by src/common/i18n.cc).
//
// Each row takes exactly two arguments:
//
//   TETHERNET_MESSAGE(identifier, English)
//
// Rules for adding a message:
//
//   1. Prefix the identifier with its module: kCommon* / kRndis* / kNet* / kUsb* /
//      kCore* / kCapi* / kCli*; CamelCase for the rest; do not paraphrase the prose.
//   2. Placeholders: implicit {} are numbered in order of appearance (0, 1, 2, ...);
//      implicit and explicit numbering must not be mixed within one string
//      (std::format's rule). Reorder with explicit indices when needed, e.g.
//      "write {1} bytes to {0}".
//   3. Insert new entries into their module's section, roughly in call order.
//
// Rule: every message's placeholders must parse cleanly and use contiguous
// indices starting at 0. Enforced entry by entry by tests/test_common_i18n.cc;
// a bad row turns `ctest -R common.i18n` red on the spot.
//
// How to add a language (the wiring deliberately stays in place with one
// language, so this is a bounded five-touch change, not a re-architecture):
//
//   1. Add a value (kSpanish = 1, say) to enum class Language in
//      tethernet/common/i18n.h, mirror it as TN_LANGUAGE_SPANISH in
//      tn_language_t (include/tethernet/capi/tethernet_c.h), and mirror it in
//      the Swift `Language` enum (gui/Sources/TethernetIPC/Localization.swift).
//   2. Give every row of this file one more argument: the macro signature
//      becomes (id, en, es) at the three extraction sites (the Msg enum in
//      i18n.h, the table(s) in src/common/i18n.cc).
//   3. Emit one more table in src/common/i18n.cc next to kMessageTable and let
//      TextIn() select it by Language.
//   4. Accept the new spelling and locale prefix in ParseLanguage() and
//      LanguageFromLocaleString().
//   5. Add the GUI menu entry and the per-key column in
//      gui/Sources/TethernetIPC/LocalizedStrings.swift, then extend the checks
//      in tests/test_common_i18n.cc and the GUI LocalizationTests so that every
//      language covers every message.

